A: Check out these blogs that describe how you can use Pex to test the Contains method:


*

*Using Pex to Prove the Correctness of the ArrayList Contains Method (Part 1)

*Using Pex to Prove the Correcteness of the ArrayList Contains Method (Part 2)
The Contains method tests whether an item is in the list, so the precondition would simply be that the list cannot be empty. After that, the test checks whether the new list contains the original item and also whether the ArrayList.Contains method correctly returns true or false.
In general, the test method might look like this (not Pex example, but common example):
[Test]
public void Contains_WhenOriginalListContainsItem_ReturnsTrue()
{
    // Arrange
    ArrayList list = new ArrayList() { 1, 2, 3 };
    int item = 2;

    // Act
    bool result = list.Contains(item);

    // Assert
    Assert.IsTrue(result);
}

The Pex version may look something like:
[PexMethod]
public void Contains_GeneralTest([PexAssumeValid]ArrayList list, [PexAssumeValid, PexFromAnySubtype] object item)
{
    // Arrange
    Assume.True(list.Count > 0); // precondition: list cannot be empty

    // Act
    bool result = list.Contains(item);

    // Assert - we'll check this manually instead of with Pex because of possible "false +ve"
    Assert.AreEqual(list.Contains(item), result);
}

Please note that Pex might sometimes not be able to refine the tests, or it might generate redundant tests, so ensure test coverage and test specifics are fine-tuned by the developer.
This blog also explains why it might be beneficial to manually craft the tests Pex is not as good at refining: http://blogs.msdn.com/b/pex/archive/2009/04/13/how-pex-runs-a-test-suite-that-you-write.aspx
