A: Here you can have the general idea how to use Pex for contract testing

public void ArrayLengthContractTest([PexMethodUnderTest]Action methodUnderTest, [PexAssumeValid]int[] arr){
  // setup
  int oldLength = arr.Length;

  // exercise
  methodUnderTest(arr);

  // verify
  PexAssume.IsNotNull(arr);
  PexAssert.IsTrue(arr.Length == oldLength);
}

In this contract testing method, you are assuming the initial array length is valid (you will need to change [PexAssumeValid]int[] arr to [PexAssumeValid]int before and [PexAssumeUnderTest]Action methodUnderTest to [PexMethodUnderTest]Action methodUnderTest):

The [PexAssumeValid] attribute tells Pex to assume that the specified parameter (and any others that aren't too complex) is valid. If you have complex objects, you can assume valid states by simply stating that they can happen.

In the verification step, you are using the PexAssert.IsTrue method that verifies that the result of the exercise. According to contract, the length of the array should be the same as the initial length after the exercise method.

But, if you need to test specific contract for ArrayList you will need to use other attributes and possibly also include the class in your test method:

public void ArrayListLengthTest([PexMethodUnderTest]ArrayList methodUnderTest, [PexAssumeValid]ArrayList list, [PexAssumeValid]ArrayList otherList){
  // setup
  int oldCount = list.Count;

  // exercise
  methodUnderTest(otherList);

  // verify
  Assume.IsNotNull(list);
  Assume.IsNotNull(otherList);
  Assert.AreEqual(list.Count, oldCount);
}

With this scenario, please note that [PexAssumeValid]ArrayList otherList was introduced to give Pex something to operate with. Without some parameter to operate with, Pex does not know what to do.

Remember that Pex will produce not only a huge amount of possible inputs/outputs, but will also generate different executions order, data flow through methods and so on. This is why the contracts you write may not always catch every logical error, but may catch many less obvious ones.
Another thing is, Pex tries to be smart to reduce the amount of tests. But there will always be some edge cases it will not test. Be aware and don't be shocked when it says that something is not tested as Pex assumes that something is fine by default and tries to "prove" that is wrong.
Hope it helped and good luck with it,

Raju.

A: Code contracts provide a way to reason about parts of your software without requiring complex formal methods. It provides a way to annotate types with code contracts and ensure that properties hold true when the code is executed. Here is example of how you can write code contracts in your arrayList,

[ContractClass(typeof(ArrayListContract))]
public interface IArrayList
{
   void Add(object item);
   void Clear();
}

[ContractClassFor(typeof(IArrayList))]
public abstract class ArrayListContract : IArrayList
{
    public void Add--------------------            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("#AssertionIfReturnValueIsFalse", New_Ret ? "NoAssertion Needed" : "Debug.Assert(" + ((New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1) ? "true" : "false") + ");");
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$AssertionIfReturnValueIsFalse", (New_Ret) ? "NoAssertion Needed" : "Debug.Assert(" + ((New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1) ? "true" : "false") + ");");

            // assert that if x is present, it must be in new list and index is either old or new index of x
            if (Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1)
            {
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$OldOrNew_indexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 ? Old_arrListIndexOfX : New_arrListIndexOfX);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$OldOrNew_lastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1 ? Old_arrListLastIndexOfX : New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
                Assert.IsTrue(New_Ret && (Old_arrListIndexOfX == New_arrListIndexOfX || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX == New_arrListLastIndexOfX));
            }

            // if x is present in old list, then it must be present in new list
            if (New_Ret)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX != -1 || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX != -1);
            }

            //if x isn't in list, no index will be found in new list
            if (!New_Ret)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1 && New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);
            }
        }
    }
}

Please note, to make use of PexObserve you must include it in your test settings as per the link provided by James McDaniel. 
[link to PexObserve] (https://github.com/Microsoft/PartialEvaluation/blob/master/VisualStudioTools/src/NUnit/PexObserve.cs)

Also, note that PexObserve works in NUnit 2.6.2 and Visual Studio 2012 RC with Partial Evaluation 1.0 (Build v4.0.30319) which may not be available in other versions. Make sure your visual studio and NUnit is updated, especially when dealing with contract testing.
