A: Here is the problem description from the Test Studio which is the framework used for automatic testing of managed code:

POST-CONDITIONS:
  
  
*
  
*If the return value is false, the last position of 'x' in new list is exactly '-1'.
  
*If 'x' is not in the list, then 'Contains(x)' should return false.
  
*If 'x' is already present in the list, then 'Contains(x)' should return true.
  
*'Count' should remain unchanged in 'Contains(x)'.
  

Here is the equivalent Pex test method using the same assertion:

public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
    int old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);
    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;

    int new_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int new_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that If the return value is false, last position of 'x' in new list is exactly '-1'
    Debug.Assert(!New_Ret || new_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1, "...some text...");

    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that If 'x' is not in the list, then 'Contains(x)' should return false
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret || old_arrListIndexOfX == -1, "...some text...");

    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that If 'x' is already present in the list, then 'Contains(x)' should return true
    Debug.Assert(!(old_arrListIndexOfX < 0 && New_Ret), "...some text...");

    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that 'Count' should remain unchanged in 'Contains(x)'
    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == arrList.Count, "...some text...");
}

Please replace the "...some text..." parts with any description and it should work. This code does exactly what is described in POST-CONDITIONS which were made on how the PUT test would be.
Please note, however, that Test Studio is outdated and not supported by Microsoft anymore. You might consider using more modern code coverage tools (such as Pex, Moles, Coyote) instead for more realistic scenarios.
Cheers.
