In our above example, we made explicit use of PexObserve and are not using any PexAssume since this is a test case specific to the particular array list contract checks. 

It's clear that our PexUnit test script does a fine job covering the contract expectations for the ArrayList. Thus, it's feasible and efficient to perform code-coverage analysis and verification tests on such a script. 

## Test Case Execution
To execute our test cases, we'll utilize Microsoft's TestCase Exporter and Import for Visual Studio. This plugin will generate test results that can be viewed in JUnit format by Moqa Labs for easy analysis. It will also integrate with popular Continuous-Integration services, such as Jenkins, so long-term coverage results can be stored and shared.

## Conclusion
Unit testing with Pex and QuickCheck provide valuable insights into test coverage by validating whether the generated tests provide a sufficient coverage of the code's behaviors. When combined with contract-driven tests, these tools can help pinpoint the code's weaknesses more effectively. Pex and QuickCheck can generate tests that are specific to the given method’s contracts, providing a wealth of insights into the method's behavior and ensuring that all possible scenarios are tested. Thus these techniques help in generating more effective and comprehensive tests.


